# Line endings are the repository's business, not each machine's.
#
# Without this file, correctness depends on every contributor having
# core.autocrlf set the way the original author happened to have it. A CRLF
# reconnect.sh reaches Linux with a shebang reading `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`,
# and `env` then looks for an interpreter literally named `bash\r`.
* text=auto

# The shell scripts are executed on Linux, so they are LF in the working tree
# too -- not only in the committed blob.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Line-oriented files, pinned in the working tree as well as in the committed
# blob for the same reason the scripts are: what you read locally should be what
# the repository holds, not whatever core.autocrlf turned it into.
#
# These are read by git and by a person, not by the scripts. An earlier version
# of this comment said the opposite -- "read by the scripts rather than by an
# editor" -- which is a rationale a later edit would reason from and get wrong:
# the file reconnect.sh actually parses is config.env, which is untracked and so
# out of .gitattributes' reach entirely, and a CRLF one is tolerated anyway --
# config_value() trims `[:space:]` from both ends of a value and a carriage
# return is in that class. config.env.example is the file a person copies and
# edits.
#
# That clause used to credit load_config() with stripping the CRLF. It does not,
# and saying so here was the same stale attribution that had to be corrected in
# reconnect.sh's own BOM comment: the `line="${line%$'\r'}"` statement it
# described was deleted once it turned out to change the outcome of no input,
# and naming the wrong function invites the next reader to narrow
# config_value()'s trim on the belief that CRLF is handled somewhere else.
manual-setup/config.env.example text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
